xt="Cornerstone: Progress Monitoring and Assessment" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.6iVPb4rWCGH" displaytext="Cornerstone Pockets: Kindergarten" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.63zbADf0rw1" displaytext="Monitor Oral Language" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.5WV3yT9nu8i" displaytext="Games and Activities" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.5ognM7gQNpQ" displaytext="Projects" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.6k0sL0SlLTN" displaytext="Formative Assessment" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.6bxGsMKvki8" displaytext="Cornerstone Pockets: Grades 1-5" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.5mIjAtkzd5u" displaytext="Before You Go On" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.5kZdwnE5ojq" displaytext="Comprehension Checks " expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.69C9oQorNvN" displaytext="Think It Over" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.6hpY6ysStGL" displaytext="Put It All Together " expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.5k3OBkiZ2L7" displaytext="Formative Assessments" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.6HGThdx9jSo" displaytext="Instructional Supports " expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.6CqgbD5AC4D" displaytext="Pearson English Learning System: Assessment" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.5wkdVlvChSW" displaytext="Stanford English Language Proficiency Test 2 (SELP 2)" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xID4MhkE0B.5axnP9r4vn6" displaytext="Quick Check" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.5xL3wpYDLTT" displaytext="In Practice" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.5xL3wpYDLTT.62OZQmqv2ph" displaytext="AIMSweb Video" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xL3wpYDLTT.6iMZB4DdGta" displaytext="Reflection Questions" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.5YC5L9XKEX3" displaytext="Apply" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.5YC5L9XKEX3.5npwioXtQty" displaytext="Assessment of Learning" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.5YC5L9XKEX3.6rD0NKOonwy" displaytext="Progress Monitoring and Assessment Activity" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5YC5L9XKEX3.5jenpQhhuti" displaytext="Cornerstone Point-of-Use Activity" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.5YC5L9XKEX3.6Ao2mUgjzp2" displaytext="Discussion Board Forum" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5YC5L9XKEX3.65XkvjYfHhe" displaytext="Reflect" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5YC5L9XKEX3.6hQWJJHIS49" displaytext="Looking Forward" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.5YC5L9XKEX3.6RBm0yq2Sxt" displaytext="Conclusion" expand="true" /></links></slidelink></links></slidelink></links></outline><search><slidetext slideid="6e8N0jGLKi8.5cmUg4Az6kb" slidebank="false">pearson english learning system using cornerstone © 2013  session 4: progress monitoring  and assessment </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6e8N0jGLKi8.5eF0fviJ9Yg" slidebank="false">introduction </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6e8N0jGLKi8.5nlZGH5ZWB6" slidebank="false">participant workbook you will be answering questions and completing activities that help deepen your understanding of integrating instruction. these questions and activities are included in the participant workbook that you should have downloaded before session 1. click here to download your  participant workbook </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6e8N0jGLKi8.5dDDnKwUr5X" slidebank="false">session 3 review in session 3, you explored the professional development that provides an instructional framework in the program. you looked at the resources to monitor progress and assess student learning. #29 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6e8N0jGLKi8.6lEagA5aBrz" slidebank="false">session 3 review review the responses on the discussion board forum from session 3.  what connections between instructional support and professional development are supported in cornerstone to address specific language development needs for english learners?   what resources provided in cornerstone accelerate students’ academic achievement? take a moment to reflect on these questions in your participant workbook.  #29 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6e8N0jGLKi8.5bAsNBv7bUn" slidebank="false">reflect on the reading assignment from session 3. how are aimsweb® and selp 2 part of the pearson english learning system? how can these additional resources help you with progress monitoring and assessment?  think about how these resources can help you with your instruction.  session 3 review </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6e8N0jGLKi8.69CLRjMreVM" slidebank="false">session 4 content objectives you will be able to: identify the instructional supports that help you with assessment. be aware of the formative and summative assessment options in the program. learn about aimsweb and how it can be used as a progress monitoring assessment tool. understand how the stanford english language proficiency test 2 (selp 2) can be used to assess students.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6e8N0jGLKi8.6L5FbEiGXWC" slidebank="false">session 4 language objectives you will be able to: write and share examples of progress monitoring and assessment in cornerstone. explain how aimsweb and selp 2 are incorporated into the pearson english learning system.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5nbNKmPhPi9" slidebank="false">formative and summative assessment assessment provides student data that  impact instructional decision making   help educators better understand which english learner strategies are best for the learner and the appropriate level of content the student is ready to receive provide information on students’ academic skill levels   give additional data on students’ language proficiency skills in reading, writing, listening, and speaking assist in determining areas of remediation before high stakes tests enable educators to understand the comparison of skill and performance of english learners with similar language development  allow educators to provide more accurate differentiated instruction  why is assessment important with english learners? </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5fIQutrEfRM" slidebank="false">formative and summative assessment after the viewing the video, answer the questions in your participant workbook. click here to play the movie #30  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5uzFhvmUZsD" slidebank="false">cornerstone: progress monitoring and assessment complete the t-chart graphic organizer in your participant workbook as you watch and learn. complete the sections appropriate to your specific grade level. #30 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6iVPb4rWCGH" slidebank="false">cornerstone pockets: kindergarten </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.63zbADf0rw1" slidebank="false">monitor oral language  monitor oral language boxes contain short mini lessons to help with oral language development that embed activities to check for student understanding. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5WV3yT9nu8i" slidebank="false">games and activities  games and activities provide opportunities to assess students’ mastery of the new material through observation of their completion of the games and activities. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5ognM7gQNpQ" slidebank="false">projects  projects offer a great way for students to demonstrate their understanding of the content they have learned and transfer their knowledge to the real world. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6k0sL0SlLTN" slidebank="false">formative assessment listening speaking reading writing the assessment book has a unit-by-unit check of students in the following categories:  listening  speaking  reading  writing </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6bxGsMKvki8" slidebank="false">cornerstone: grades 1-5   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5mIjAtkzd5u" slidebank="false">before you go on  before you go on in read allows you check in with students to ensure that they have understood what they are reading.  students can also participate in on your own activities where they discuss their responses to these questions and then share them with the entire class.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5kZdwnE5ojq" slidebank="false">comprehension checks   comprehension checks reinforce specific reading strategies and help to assess students’ understanding of each piece of informational or literacy text in the unit. in grades 1-5, students complete graphic organizers to demonstrate their understanding of the specific reading strategy associated with the text.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.69C9oQorNvN" slidebank="false">think it over  think it over allows students to recall, comprehend, and analyze what they have read in the story. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6hpY6ysStGL" slidebank="false">put it all together  put it all together contains activities that check for student understanding of the content they learned in the unit.  link the readings activities assess students’ understanding of the unit theme.   discussion provides opportunities for students to integrate new vocabulary words into their discussions and revisit the big question to check for understanding.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5k3OBkiZ2L7" slidebank="false">formative assessments list examples of formative assessments in cornerstone in your participant workbook. diagnostic pretest reading tests unit tests midterm test posttest rubric and answer keys #31  diagnostic pretest  reading tests  unit tests  midterm test  posttest  rubric and answer keys </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6HGThdx9jSo" slidebank="false">instructional supports  cornerstone provides instructional supports and metacognitive activities that help students be more successful when taking formative assessments.  leveled support  boxes grammar  checks learning  strategies #32  leveled support boxes provide built-in leveled support throughout the unit that has been differentiated according to various levels of language proficiency. this additional support allows learners to demonstrate their understanding of major concepts presented in the unit. leveled support  boxes  grammar checks in grades 1-5 reinforce grammar and check for understanding and mastery.  grammar  checks  learning strategies provide additional practice to better help students understand and retain the content of a reading selection. practice activities in learning strategies in grades 1-5 also offer opportunities to assess students’ abilities to meet the objectives in the unit. learning  strategies </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6CqgbD5AC4D" slidebank="false">pearson english learning system: assessment </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6BWFQUQQd7g" slidebank="false">pearson english learning system: assessment the pearson english learning system informs instruction with new assessment tools that include aimsweb and selp 2 provides assessments to measure student mastery, readiness for learning, proficiency, and progress  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5Ya4ndsCurK" slidebank="false">pearson english learning system: assessment the pearson english learning system helps schools evaluate instructional efficacy by monitoring groups of students who are getting similar instruction. you can look at the data over time and evaluate your program’s effectiveness. provides a report that helps educators determine if an english learner student at a given proficiency level is performing and growing, higher than, lower than, or similar to other english learners within the same levels of english proficiency.  now, let’s look a little closer at each of these vital assessment and progress monitoring tools that you can use with cornerstone.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6e5723mtKMS" slidebank="false">pearson english learning system: assessment #32 answer the question in your participant workbook.  the stanford english language proficiency test 2 or selp 2 is a comprehensive, standardized test that measures english language proficiency in reading, writing, speaking and listening and is typically used to provide additional student data and to guide language instruction.   aimsweb is a universal screening and academic progress monitoring system based on brief direct, frequent, and continuous student assessment. it shows student achievement and growth in relation to targets for performance. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5wkdVlvChSW" slidebank="false">stanford english language proficiency test 2 (selp 2)      click here to learn more about the stanford english language proficiency test 2 or selp 2. record your key learnings about the selp 2 in the participant workbook.  #32  let’s look in more detail at the stanford english language proficiency test 2 or selp 2: part of the pearson english learning system and helps address english learners’ english language proficiency needs  allows school to more quickly and accurately place ell students in grades k-12 monitors english language proficiency progress throughout the school years distinguishes students’ academic and social language capabilities valid, reliable, research-based with normative ell data informed by tesol, wida, and ccss   the selp 2 is part of the pearson english learning system and helps address english learners’ english language proficiency needs  allows school to more quickly and accurately place ell students in grades k-12 monitors english language proficiency progress throughout the school years distinguishes students’ academic and social language capabilities provides valid, reliable, research-based with normative  ell data is informed by tesol, wida, and ccss  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6DNE8F7poNp" slidebank="false">stanford english language proficiency test 2 (selp 2) the stanford english language proficiency test 2 includes new academic language items that measure the student’s ability to learn by gauging comprehension of english language content, vocabulary, grammar, and discourse.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6cpXXc50r2z" slidebank="false">stanford english language proficiency test 2 (selp 2) lessons from cornerstone are correlated to the subtest/clusters of skills that are being measured by the stanford english language proficiency test 2. these subtest/clusters include listening, speaking, reading, writing conventions, and writing. you will find these correlations in appendix b of your teacher’s edition.   the curriculum and the selp 2 links in this resource will guide you to the correct place in the curriculum to target your instruction. throughout cornerstone, you will find these point-of-use references relate to specific the stanford english language proficiency test 2 assessments that can be used to assess students’ proficiency in a specific area.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.5axnP9r4vn6" slidebank="false">quick check to review the content covered in this learn section, please select the statements that are true on the next page.  record your key learnings about the selp 2 in your participant workbook.   #32 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xID4MhkE0B.6E2Tjhq96hq" slidebank="false">based on the information you have learned, which of the following are key reasons to incorporate the stanford english language proficiency test 2 into your cornerstone program?  allows school to more quickly and accurately place ell students in grades k-12 monitors english language proficiency progress throughout the school year distinguishes students’ academic and social language capabilities includes three subtest/clusters that are reading, writing, and language arts quick check valid, reliable, research-based with normative ell data informed by tesol, wida, and ccss #32  continue  continue  try again </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xL3wpYDLTT.62OZQmqv2ph" slidebank="false">aimsweb video let’s learn more about aimsweb. when the video finishes, answer the questions in your participant workbook. #32      click here to play the movie  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xL3wpYDLTT.6iMZB4DdGta" slidebank="false">video reflections questions after viewing the video, respond to the following content knowledge video reflection questions in your participant workbook: how can selp 2 and aimsweb assist in monitoring progress and assessment in cornerstone?   #33 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5YC5L9XKEX3.5npwioXtQty" slidebank="false">assessment of learning in this next section, you will complete activities to assess your knowledge of the pearson english learning system assessment tools. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5YC5L9XKEX3.6rD0NKOonwy" slidebank="false">progress monitoring and assessment activity look at a unit in your teacher’s edition. complete the graphic organizer in the participant workbook, and record all the examples of progress monitoring and assessment related to that specific unit. #33 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5YC5L9XKEX3.5jenpQhhuti" slidebank="false">cornerstone point-of-use activity choose a unit in your teacher’s edition. in your participant workbook, record how you can use aimsweb and selp 2 to assess student learning at point of use in the program.  #34 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5YC5L9XKEX3.6Ao2mUgjzp2" slidebank="false">discussion board forum the discussion board forum provides an opportunity for you to discuss ideas and strategies with your colleagues. remember, as with any discussion, the communication must go two ways. as you post your responses on the discussion board, be sure to comment on your peers’ posts as well so that there is continuous conversation. once you have completed your assignment, check back often to help keep the discussion going.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5YC5L9XKEX3.65XkvjYfHhe" slidebank="false">reflect now, share your thoughts!  respond to the session 4 questions on the discussion board: reflect on this session. refer to a lesson and identify the assessment component. describe how aimsweb and selp 2 support you in reaching realistic instructional goals for english learners. how does progress monitoring support you to assess an individual learner’s needs?  share your response to these questions on the discussion board and respond to two other posts. #35 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5YC5L9XKEX3.6hQWJJHIS49" slidebank="false">looking forward work on the following tasks before beginning live session 5.  reading assignment      click on the link to read the common core state standards for ela and ells.      click on the link to review the  comparative proficiency levels  chart for cornerstone pockets or  cornerstone grades 1-5.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5YC5L9XKEX3.6RBm0yq2Sxt" slidebank="false">congratulations on completing session 4! </slidetext></search></nav_data><resource_data description="&lt;p align='left'&gt;&lt;font face='Arial' size='11' color='#FFFFFF'&gt;Here are some useful links and documents:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" /><transcript_data /><glossary_data /></bwFrame>